---
import GuideLayout from "@/layouts/GuideLayout.astro";
import { GUIDES_BY_SLUG } from "@/data/guides";
const g = GUIDES_BY_SLUG["how-many-work-hours-in-a-year"]!;
---

<GuideLayout title={g.title} metaTitle={g.metaTitle} description={g.description} slug={g.slug} updated={g.updated}>
  <p>
    A standard full-time work year is <strong>2,080 hours</strong>: 40 hours a
    week &times; 52 weeks. That is the figure used to convert salaries to hourly
    rates and to plan headcount. But the hours you actually work are usually
    fewer, and the number of working days shifts from year to year.
  </p>

  <h2>The standard: 2,080 hours</h2>
  <p><code>40 hours/week &times; 52 weeks = 2,080 hours</code></p>
  <p>
    Divide an annual salary by 2,080 for the equivalent hourly rate:
    <code>$60,000 &divide; 2,080 &asymp; $28.85</code>. Multiply an hourly rate
    by 2,080 for the annual figure. The full method and its limits are in
    <a href="/guides/convert-hourly-wage-to-annual-salary/">converting an hourly
    wage to an annual salary</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>The 2,087-hour version</h2>
  <p>
    The US federal government uses <strong>2,087 hours</strong> as its official
    work-year divisor. It is the average once you account for the fact that the
    calendar repeats on a 28-year cycle with a mix of 260, 261, and 262
    weekday years. For most private-sector purposes 2,080 is close enough;
    2,087 matters for precise federal hourly-rate calculations.
  </p>

  <h2>Working days by year</h2>
  <p>
    Count only Monday&ndash;Friday dates and the total moves between roughly 260
    and 262, depending on how weekends fall and whether it is a leap year.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Year</th><th>Weekdays (Mon&ndash;Fri)</th><th>Hours at 8/day</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>2025</td><td>261</td><td>2,088</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2026</td><td>261</td><td>2,088</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2027</td><td>261</td><td>2,088</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2028</td><td>260</td><td>2,080</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2029</td><td>261</td><td>2,088</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    These exclude public holidays. Subtract your country's holidays — typically
    8&ndash;13 days in the US, 8 in England and Wales — for actual working days.
  </p>

  <h2>Hours you actually work</h2>
  <p><code>hours worked = 2,080 &minus; (PTO days + holidays) &times; hours per workday</code></p>
  <p>
    With 15 vacation days and 10 public holidays at 8 hours each, that is
    <code>2,080 &minus; 200 = 1,880 hours</code>. Add sick days and the real
    figure for a typical US full-time job lands around 1,850&ndash;1,900
    productive hours a year.
  </p>

  <h2>Part-time and other schedules</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Hours/week</th><th>Per year (52 wk)</th><th>Per month</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>40</td><td>2,080</td><td>173.33</td></tr>
      <tr><td>37.5</td><td>1,950</td><td>162.50</td></tr>
      <tr><td>35</td><td>1,820</td><td>151.67</td></tr>
      <tr><td>32</td><td>1,664</td><td>138.67</td></tr>
      <tr><td>30</td><td>1,560</td><td>130.00</td></tr>
      <tr><td>25</td><td>1,300</td><td>108.33</td></tr>
      <tr><td>20</td><td>1,040</td><td>86.67</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Expressed as a share of full-time, 30 hours a week is a
    <a href="/guides/what-is-full-time-equivalent/">0.75 FTE</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Hours in a month, quarter, and other periods</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Month:</strong> 2,080 &divide; 12 = <strong>173.33 hours</strong>. Individual months range from about 160 (a 20-workday month) to 184 (a 23-workday month).</li>
    <li><strong>Quarter:</strong> roughly <strong>520 hours</strong> (13 weeks &times; 40).</li>
    <li><strong>Business days in a month:</strong> usually 20&ndash;23.</li>
    <li><strong>Work weeks in a year:</strong> 52 (plus one or two extra days), which is why a biweekly payroll sometimes has a <a href="/guides/biweekly-vs-semimonthly-pay/">27th paycheck</a>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Why it matters</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Salary comparisons:</strong> a job expecting 45-hour weeks has a lower real hourly rate than one at 40.</li>
    <li><strong>Contractor rates:</strong> contractors have no paid time off, so they need a higher rate to match an equivalent salary — and they rarely bill a full 2,080 hours.</li>
    <li><strong>Project planning:</strong> a full-time employee provides roughly 1,850&ndash;1,900 productive hours a year, not 2,080.</li>
    <li><strong>Accrual rates:</strong> PTO that accrues "per hour worked" is based on actual hours, so it is a little under the headline entitlement.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
  <h3>Is a work year 2,080 or 2,087 hours?</h3>
  <p>
    2,080 for everyday salary-to-hourly math; 2,087 is the US federal
    government's precise average across the 28-year calendar cycle.
  </p>
  <h3>How many hours is full-time?</h3>
  <p>
    Commonly 40 a week, but employers may set 35 or 37.5. The US Affordable Care
    Act uses 30 hours a week as its full-time line for employer requirements.
  </p>

  <h2>Calculate it for your schedule</h2>
  <p>
    The <a href="/calculators/working-hours-calculator/">Working Hours
    Calculator</a> takes your hours per week, weeks per year, PTO, and holidays
    and returns hours per week, month, and year, plus working days and
    full-time equivalent. The
    <a href="/calculators/salary-to-hourly-calculator/">Salary to Hourly
    Calculator</a> uses it to convert pay accurately.
  </p>

